<section id="how-it-works" class="container section">
  <div class="two-col">
    <div>
      <h2>Three gods, one job each</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      {# The Scarce Gold Rule: gold marks a payoff, so only the three bosses
         keep it here. The enabler cards are ordinary card links (.clink), which
         is what the combos page already uses for inline references. #}
      <p>Revealed in hand, each boss adds a different piece: <a class="gold" href="{{ '/sacred-beasts/cards/' | url }}#uria" data-img="{{ '/assets/cards/sacred-beasts/uria.jpg' | url }}">Uria</a> grabs a Sacred Beast Trap, <a class="gold" href="{{ '/sacred-beasts/cards/' | url }}#hamon" data-img="{{ '/assets/cards/sacred-beasts/hamon.jpg' | url }}">Hamon</a> grabs a Sacred Beast Spell, <a class="gold" href="{{ '/sacred-beasts/cards/' | url }}#raviel" data-img="{{ '/assets/cards/sacred-beasts/raviel.jpg' | url }}">Raviel</a> grabs another Sacred Beast monster. None of the three can be Normal Summoned, so getting one onto the field needs a Sacred Beast card doing the work: <a class="clink" href="{{ '/sacred-beasts/cards/' | url }}#fallen-paradise-of-the-sacred-beasts" data-img="{{ '/assets/cards/sacred-beasts/fallen-paradise-of-the-sacred-beasts.jpg' | url }}">Fallen Paradise of the Sacred Beasts</a>, <a class="clink" href="{{ '/sacred-beasts/cards/' | url }}#sacred-beasts-combined-assault" data-img="{{ '/assets/cards/sacred-beasts/sacred-beasts-combined-assault.jpg' | url }}">Sacred Beasts Combined Assault</a>, or a second boss's own summon effect.</p>
      <p>Feed three Level 10s that can't be Normal Summoned into <a class="gold" href="{{ '/sacred-beasts/cards/' | url }}#chaotic-phantasmal-sacred-beasts" data-img="{{ '/assets/cards/sacred-beasts/chaotic-phantasmal-sacred-beasts.jpg' | url }}">The Chaotic Phantasmal Sacred Beasts</a> and you end on a 5000/5000 that survives its first two destructions each turn and negates a monster's effects up to three times a turn.</p>
      <p>Uria, Hamon, and Raviel debuted two decades earlier, and bring a lot of nostalgia with them. Despite looking like shadow versions of the Egyptian Gods, their names are actually Judeo-Christian: Uria is a spelling of Uriah, Hamon comes from the Book of Enoch, and Raviel is a spelling of Raziel.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

